course: dictionary
lesson: 14853
title: Rapidly: very quickly
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rapidly means very quickly, at a great speed. It comes from the word rapid, and the -ly on the end turns it into an adverb, a word that tells us how something happens.
explain: Look at the clues around it. «The fire spread rapidly through the dry grass.» The fire did not spread slowly or gently. Spread is about moving, so rapidly tells us how fast it moved. Swap in quickly and check: the sentence still makes sense, so you have the right meaning.
explain: Rapidly can also mean happening in a short time. «The town grew rapidly after the new railway opened.» Grew shows a change, and rapidly tells us the change happened in a short time, not over many years.
fact: Rapidly means very quickly, at a great speed.
fact: Rapidly is an adverb, made from the adjective rapid.
fact: «Water boils rapidly» when it is heated very hard.
fact: Rapidly can also mean happening in a short time, as in «the town grew rapidly».
fact: Useful phrases to remember: «grow rapidly», «move rapidly», «rapidly increasing».
exercise: «The runner moved rapidly down the track.» What does rapidly mean? | Sadly | Quietly | *Very quickly | Slowly and carefully
exercise: «Her heart beat rapidly when the lights went out.» What does rapidly tell us about the heartbeat? | Late | Loudly | Gently | *Fast
exercise: Which word is the adjective that rapidly comes from? | slow | rapidity | hard | *rapid
test: «Fire spreads rapidly in dry grass.» Why is rapidly a useful word in this sentence? | It tells us the grass is green | *It tells us how fast the fire spreads | It tells us who lit the fire | It tells us the colour of the fire
test: Which phrase means something is getting bigger in a short time? | Gently falling | Very slowly sleeping | Quietly sitting | *Rapidly increasing
exam: Ella says rapidly only means «in a short time». Is she fully right? | *No, it means very quickly, at a great speed, and it can also mean in a short time | Yes, that is the only meaning | Yes, it means very loudly | No, it means very slowly
